  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 127 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe advertised reprieve from moisture will be a short lived one as \nthe frontolytic boundary and envelope of associated moisture lift \nback across the region during the day on Wednesday into Thursday as \nan expansive area of surface high pressure becomes established \nacross the southeastern United States. Northeasterly surface flow \nwill gradually veer to an easterly direction by Thursday as the axis \nof surface ridging across the southeastern United States slides \neastward into the western Atlantic waters. This will focus the best \ndiurnal rain chances across the western half of the peninsula during \nthe second half of the work week, although overnight and morning \nactivity will still be a possibility along the east coast of South \nFlorida given the breezy easterly flow and continued moisture \nadvection. Mid-level ridging briefly established across the \nsoutheastern United States during the early to mid portion of the \nwork week will quickly flatten by Thursday as the next lobe of \ntroughing slides eastward across the northern United States. As the \npositively tilted trough amplifies and digs across the eastern \nUnited States, a strengthening surface low will strengthen and push \neast-northeastward in tandem. Model guidance remains in agreement \nthat a surface frontal boundary attached to the aforementioned \nsurface low will push southwards across the eastern United States in \nresponse and arrive in the vicinity of the Florida Peninsula during \nthe upcoming weekend. There has been a great amount of variability/ \nuncertainty in model solutions of exact frontal boundary placement \n(stall, full passage, etc) from run to run and model member to model \nmember (ensemble vs deterministic). While forecast details for the \nupcoming weekend will surely become clearer as we progress further \ninto the work week, let's briefly delve into what a stall of the \nboundary to the north could transpire for our area as opposed to a \nfull frontal passage. A stall of the boundary to the north of South \nFlorida would place the region in the envelope of deep tropical \nmoisture which could facilitate higher than normal rain chances and \na continuation of a humid and tropical airmass. The full passage of \nthe frontal boundary to the south of the region could usher in \nanother push of continental dry air into South Florida and daytime \nforecast temperature near or slightly below average. Cluster \nanalysis still depicts variations in model guidance on the \ntiming/amplitude of the lobe of troughing across the eastern United \nStates during this period, which could have implications on whether \nthe boundary will stall or make a full sweep across the region.\n\nForecast high temperatures will remain status quo through the end of \nthe work week with values in the upper 80s to low 90s possible each \nafternoon. The return of moisture advection across the region on \nWednesday will result in a gradual increase of nocturnal \ntemperatures across the region, with a possible return to minimum \ntemperatures in the low 80s along the immediate east coast late in\nthe week.",
